  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "What is the amplitude of $f(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x?$",
  "worked_solution": "To plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- Then, stretch it parallel to the $y$-axis by a stretch factor of $4.$\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14255908/assets/52177b289595a66bb5846d469c070db74371690fc3e321517c6e7c7658794da3.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the maximum value is ${y}_{max}=4$ and the minimum value is ${y}_{min}=-4.$ Therefore, the amplitude is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Amplitude } & =\\frac{{y}_{max}-{y}_{min}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-(-4)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+4}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$",
